# Attempt 3: Arch — Review

Arch is one of the best distros I've ever used. It has everything I was
looking for: simple yet complex, and it trusts the user to know what they're
doing with their system, letting them use it fully without restrictions.

It's stable while also providing frequent updates. During installation, it
lets you pick from various desktop environments. On top of that, the Arch
Wiki has a huge amount of useful information; reading through it alone
teaches you a lot about Linux in general. I'd recommend reading it even if
you have no plans to install Arch, just to learn.

Even if you don't want to read the wiki and just want to run Arch,
`archinstall` has made installing it much easier and simpler. You really
only need to learn how to flash a USB with the ISO and check its hash after
downloading; the rest of the steps are fairly straightforward. Since Arch is
lightweight, it can be installed on almost any low-end device and still run
well.

I'd definitely recommend it to intermediate Linux users who want to learn
more about the OS, and to people with an old or low-end laptop struggling to
run Windows 11 or another heavy OS.

It's only been a couple of days since I installed Arch, but I'm loving how
it feels, similar to Fedora, and I can't wait to learn more about it.
Hopefully, in the future, I'll be able to install it manually without
relying on third-party guides, other than the wiki itself. Arch has a
reputation for being difficult, and using it successfully has made me feel a
lot more confident with Linux in general. I've been loving discovering new
things and learning as I go.

**Rating: 9/10**
